Lesson 1: Introduction to the Web + Basic HTML Structure
________________________________________
Course: Web Development Basics
Grade Level: Beginners (Age 10+ or adult learners)
Duration: 45–60 minutes
Lesson Number: Day 1
Topic: websites and Basic structure


1. Learning Objectives
By the end of this lesson, students will be able to:
•	What is a website
•	Identify the websites that they are using
•	What is a Browser, Server, URL
•	What is Frontend and Backend(basics) 
•	Why are we using HTML
•	What is the difference between HTML, CSS, and JavaScript in Web Development

2. Materials Required
•	Computer / Laptop
•	Online HTML Editors / IDEs:
o	Replit (https://replit.com)
o	CodePen (https://codepen.io)
o	JSFiddle (https://jsfiddle.net)
o	W3Schools Tryit Editor (https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp)
•	Slides (optional)
•	Teacher’s notes & sample code

3. Lesson Overview (Teacher Notes)
In this lesson, students will learn the basics of how the web works and understand the purpose of HTML as the foundation of all web pages. They will explore the structure of an HTML document and create their first simple webpage using basic tags. By the end of the lesson, students should be able to write and run a basic HTML page.
4. Lesson Breakdown
	4.1 Warm-Up (5 minutes)
Ask students
Which websites are the ones they are familiar with? (If they are not familiar, then give one or two examples)
Why are we using those websites? (Purpose)

	4.2 Introduction to Website Structure (10 minutes)
Explain in simple terms:
The structure of a webpage- use a real-world example like the human body
Introduce HTML, CSS, JavaScript
	HTML (bone or skeleton)-HTML(Hyper Text Markup Language) gives structure and shape to the webpage, just like the skeleton gives structure to the human body.

	CSS (Skin, Hair, Clothes & Appearance)-CSS(Cascading Stylesheet) controls the look and style of the webpage, just like skin, hair, and clothes give a human their appearance.


	JAVASCRIPT (Brain & Muscles (Actions & Movement))-JavaScript adds interactivity and behavior, just like the brain and muscles allow a human to move and respond.

	4.3 Introduction to Tags and basic HTML structure (15 minutes)
Basic Structure
Guide students to type the code in any online HTML editor and run it to view the output.

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>My First Webpage</title>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Hello World!</h1>
</body>
</html>

Simply explain the following tags and Explain opening tag, closing tag, and the importance of closing tags
	<!DOCTYPE html> - It will tell the browser which version of HTML we are using
	<html>…</html> - The root tag of the webpage
	<head>…</head> - This section contains information about the webpage, not the visible content.
	<title>…</title> - The text that appears in the browser tab.
	<body>…</body>- The part of the page that contains everything the user sees.
	Explain Heading tags(h1-h6), let them try

	4.4 Hands-On Coding (10 minutes)

1)	Write HTML code to Print Student name, the school’s name, and the favourite subject on the screen, along with changing the text on the browser tab to “About Me”

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>About Me</title>
</head>

<body>
    <h1>Student Name: John Doe</h1>
    <h1>School Name: Sunshine Public School</h1>
    <h1>Favourite Subject: Mathematics</h1>
</body>
</html>

	4.5 Challenge Activity (5–10 minutes)
Assign this challenge:
HTML code for printing about their School in one paragraph (introduce Paragraph tag), ask them to include a heading also.

5. Assessment (Exit Ticket – Quick Check)
Ask students to answer:
	What is HTML, CSS, JavaScript (full form also).
	Explain the basic tags and usage.
	What is the difference between <h1> and <p>.
	Which are the different heading tags?
	Where does the <title> appear?
	Why is <body> important?

6. Homework / Extension Activity
Write an HTML code for printing about their Favourite food include heading and one paragraph
